Powerup Simulator
=================

A C++17/OpenGL learning project. The current milestone draws a colored
triangle on a purple background using GLFW and the bundled GLAD 1 loader.
It requests an OpenGL 3.3 core context and uses GLSL 330 shaders.

Build and run (from the repository root)
----------------------------------------

Requires CMake, a C/C++ compiler, GLFW development files, OpenGL development
files, and a working graphical session. No new dependencies were added.

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build -j2
    ./build/sim

Configuration generates build instructions; compilation builds the sim
executable. The existing build directory uses Unix Makefiles; keep its
generator when reconfiguring. Shader sources are embedded in src/main.cpp,
so shader edits require a rebuild. Startup prints vendor, renderer, and
OpenGL version; shader errors go to standard error and cause exit status 1.

Controls and manual checks
--------------------------

- Expect a triangle with red, green, and blue corners and interpolated
  colors inside, on a dark purple background.
- Resize the window: the viewport should fill the framebuffer and the
  triangle should remain centered. Its proportions change with the window
  aspect ratio at this milestone; no camera or projection is present yet.
- Press Escape to exit. Relaunch and check the window's close button too.
- Minimize and restore the window and confirm rendering resumes.

See PROGRESS.md for verification results and graphics environment details.

How the triangle works
----------------------

Initialization compiles two shaders and links their program, then copies
three vertices from CPU memory into a VBO. Each vertex has six floats:
x, y, z, red, green, blue. The VAO records two attributes with a stride of
6 * sizeof(float) bytes; color begins at 3 * sizeof(float) bytes.

Each frame clears the background, binds the program and VAO, and draws
three vertices. The vertex shader writes clip-space positions with w = 1.
The rasterizer interpolates vertex colors, and the fragment shader writes
those colors. Buffers, the VAO, and the program are released while the
OpenGL context still exists.

Exercise (10-20 minutes)
-----------------------

Predict what moving the top vertex to x = 0.3 will do, make the change in
the vertices array, and rebuild. Then change the three RGB colors and
compare the resulting gradient with your prediction.

1. Why is the stride six floats, while the color offset is three floats?
2. How do three vertex colors produce many colors inside the triangle?
